.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_construct \- declare and initialise a ccol_event_loop in one step
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_construct(name, max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                     num_reactor_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a ccol_event_loop variable with the name name. It
then calls ccol_event_loop_create() to initialise the variable. It
calls ccol_fatal_err() when the create fails.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                  Name of the variable for the ccol_event_loop handle.
max_events_per_wait   Size of the event batch buffer of the polling
                      thread. The value must be from 1 to INT_MAX. It
                      must also be small enough that max_events_per_wait
                      times the size of one kernel event record (struct
                      epoll_event on Linux, struct kevent on FreeBSD)
                      does not overflow size_t.
num_lock_stripes      Number of independent lock stripes for the registry
                      of fds and entries. The value must be >= 1. A
                      value of 1 means one shared lock.
num_reactor_threads   Number of background reactor threads. The value
                      must be >= 1. A value of 1 means one thread that
                      polls and dispatches in line.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro calls ccol_fatal_err() on a failure. Call
ccol_event_loop_create() or ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs()
directly when you need to handle an error gracefully.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_event_loop_construct(loop, 32, 1, 1);
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                                handlers, NULL, NULL);
ccol_event_loop_remove(loop, reg);
ccol_event_loop_destroy(loop);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_declare (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3)
